Hi Winston,

This is excellent! Thank you for that pointer.

I think building on top of this, or perhaps a better way would be to
actually cache the results to disk and then reuse when possible, since
restarting the server would clear cache.

-Sarven

On 04/10/2013 12:35 PM, Winston Chang wrote:
> Hi Sarven -
>
> Reactive expressions will only cache the most recent values -- this is
> so that if you have multiple outputs (or reactive expressions) that uses
> getData(), it won't have to run again. However, it doesn't cache old
> values from previous runs. If you want to do that, you'll have to cache
> values yourself.
>
>
> You could use the memoise package to do caching. When you pass memoise()
> a function, it will return another function that caches return values,
> based in the input.
>
> # Create a memoized version of runif()
> rr <- memoise(runif)
>
> # First run, with input 1
> rr(1)
> # [1] 0.6785051
>
> # All subsequent runs with the same input (1) return the same value, because
> # it is cached
> rr(1)
> # [1] 0.6785051
>
> # Input of 2 will run runif(2)
> rr(2)
> [1] 0.5760216 0.7925082
> rr(1)
> # [1] 0.6785051
>
>
>
> Keep in mind, though, that if you use memoise, the scope where you
> define the memoized function matters. To use it in a Shiny app so that
> each session gets its own cache, define it within the shinyServer function:
>
> shinyServer(function (input, output) {
>    # Create memoized function in scope of each session
>    rr <- memoise(runif)
>
>    output$numbers <- renderText({
>      rr(input$n)
>    })
> })
>
>
>
> If you want it to be shared across all sessions, define it outside the
> shinyServer function:
>
> # Create memoized function in scope shared across all sessions
> rr <- memoise(runif)
>
> shinyServer(function (input, output) {
>    output$numbers <- renderText({
>      rr(input$n)
>    })
> })
>
>
>
> -Winston
>
>
>
>
>
>
